# Google Cloud AI & ML Concepts: Cheat Sheet

A consolidated reference for every AI/ML concept on the Google Cloud "Discover" learning hub
(<https://cloud.google.com/discover>), grouped by theme. Definitions are faithful to Google
Cloud's own framing. A few concepts the hub links inline (transformer, embeddings, computer
vision, speech, recommendations) live under `/learn/`, `/use-cases/`, or product pages rather
than `/discover/`; those are flagged with their real URL.

> Compiled 2026-06-23. Source convention: `cloud.google.com/discover/<slug>` unless noted.

---

## 1. Foundations

| Concept | One-line definition |
|---|---|
| **Artificial intelligence (AI)** | A set of technologies that let computers learn, reason, and perform tasks that used to require human intelligence (understanding language, analysing data, making suggestions). A broad field, not a single technology, relying on data, algorithms, and compute. `/learn/what-is-artificial-intelligence` |
| **AI vs machine learning** | AI is the broad umbrella (sense, reason, act, adapt like a human); ML is a subset that learns and improves from data without explicit programming. AI is categorised by capability into ANI (narrow, today), AGI (human-level), ASI (theoretical super-intelligence). `/learn/artificial-intelligence-vs-machine-learning` |
| **Machine learning (ML)** | A subset of AI where systems autonomously learn and improve from large datasets without being explicitly programmed, getting better as more data is processed. Powers fraud detection, personalisation, translation, automated support. `/learn/what-is-machine-learning` |
| **Deep learning** | A type of ML using multi-layered artificial neural networks (inspired by the brain) where each layer learns a feature (edges, then shapes, then objects). Trained via backpropagation; powers image recognition, NLP, and text-to-image. `/discover/what-is-deep-learning` |
| **Deep learning vs ML** | Deep learning is a specialised subdivision of ML: ML can use simpler statistical models, while deep learning uses many-layered neural networks that handle complex, unstructured data with less manual feature engineering. `/discover/deep-learning-vs-machine-learning` |
| **Neural network** | A brain-inspired ML algorithm of interconnected "neurons" arranged in weighted layers; weights are tuned during training to minimise prediction error. Types: feedforward, recurrent (RNN), convolutional (CNN). `/discover/what-is-a-neural-network` |
| **Convolutional neural networks (CNNs)** | A neural-network architecture specialised for grid-like data such as images, using convolution layers to detect spatial features (edges, textures, shapes). The workhorse behind image classification and object detection. `/discover/what-are-convolutional-neural-networks` |
| **AI model** | A program trained on a large dataset to learn patterns so it can predict or decide on new, unseen data. Hierarchy: AI models (broad) contain ML models, which contain deep-learning models. Lifecycle: train, test, deploy, evaluate. `/discover/what-is-an-ai-model` |
| **Cognitive computing** | Systems that simulate human thought processes (learning, reasoning, understanding natural language) to augment human decision-making, often over large, ambiguous, unstructured data. `/discover/what-is-cognitive-computing` |
| **Artificial general intelligence (AGI)** | Hypothetical machine intelligence able to understand or learn any intellectual task a human can. Does not yet exist; distinguished by generalisation across domains and common-sense knowledge. `/discover/what-is-artificial-general-intelligence` |

