## 2. Learning paradigms

| Concept | One-line definition |
|---|---|
| **Supervised learning** | Trains on labelled data (inputs paired with correct outputs) so the model learns the input-to-output relationship. Used for risk assessment, image classification, fraud detection, recommendations. `/discover/what-is-supervised-learning` |
| **Unsupervised learning** | Learns from unlabelled data, discovering patterns and structure on its own (clustering, association). Used for anomaly detection, customer segmentation, recommendation engines. `/discover/what-is-unsupervised-learning` |
| **Supervised vs unsupervised** | Supervised needs labelled data and predicts known targets; unsupervised finds hidden structure in unlabelled data. Choice depends on whether you have labels and what you are trying to find. `/discover/supervised-vs-unsupervised-learning` |
| **Reinforcement learning (RL)** | An agent learns optimal behaviour by trial and error in an environment, guided by rewards and penalties, to maximise reward over time. Four elements: agent, environment, actions, rewards. `/discover/what-is-reinforcement-learning` |
| **Federated learning** | Trains a shared model across many decentralised devices or servers without moving the raw local data, preserving privacy and reducing bandwidth. Frameworks: TensorFlow Federated, Flower, NVIDIA FLARE, FATE. `/discover/what-is-federated-learning` |
| **Synthetic data** | Artificially generated data that mimics the statistical properties of real data, used to train or test models when real data is scarce, sensitive, or imbalanced. `/discover/what-is-synthetic-data` |

