## 3. Generative AI & large language models

| Concept | One-line definition |
|---|---|
| **Generative AI** | AI that creates new content (text, images, music, audio, video) by learning patterns in human-created data and using them to generate novel output. Powered by foundation models. `/use-cases/generative-ai` |
| **Large language models (LLMs)** | Statistical language models trained on massive text corpora, typically built on the Transformer architecture, that generate and translate text and perform NLP tasks like summarisation and Q&A. `/ai/llms` |
| **Foundation models** | Large models pre-trained on vast data (often via self-supervised learning) that adapt to many downstream tasks. Scale produces "emergent" capabilities. All LLMs are foundation models; not all foundation models are LLMs. `/discover/what-are-foundation-models` |
| **GPT (generative pre-trained transformer)** | A type of LLM using the Transformer architecture and deep learning to produce human-like text. "Pre-trained" by learning to predict the next token; self-attention layers process input in parallel. `/discover/what-is-gpt` |
| **Transformer architecture** | The neural-network design Google invented and open-sourced in 2017 that underpins modern LLMs; self-attention lets it weigh relationships between all words in a sequence simultaneously. (No standalone Discover page; covered inside the GPT and LLM pages.) |
| **Multimodal AI** | Models that process and generate across multiple data types (text, images, audio, video) in one system. Google's Gemini was built multimodal from the ground up. `/use-cases/multimodal-ai` |
| **Generative adversarial networks (GANs)** | A framework (Goodfellow, 2014) of two competing networks: a generator that fabricates realistic data and a discriminator that tries to tell real from fake. The contest drives both to improve. `/discover/what-are-generative-adversarial-networks` |
| **AI hallucinations** | Confident but incorrect or fabricated outputs from a generative model, caused by gaps or biases in training data. Mitigated by grounding techniques such as RAG and knowledge graphs. `/discover/what-are-ai-hallucinations` |

> **Embeddings / diffusion** are referenced inline but have no dedicated Discover page. Embeddings convert text/image data into numerical vectors that capture semantic meaning (used for search, clustering, RAG); the nearest hub page is **Semantic search** (Section 7). Diffusion is the technique behind much AI image generation.

