## 4. AI agents

| Concept | One-line definition |
|---|---|
| **AI agents** | Software systems that use AI to pursue goals and complete tasks for users, showing reasoning, planning, and memory with a degree of autonomy. Built on the ReAct pattern (Reasoning, Acting, Observing, Planning, Collaborating, Self-refining); each agent has a persona, memory, and tools. `/discover/what-are-ai-agents` |
| **Multi-agent system** | Multiple AI agents that coordinate, delegate, and collaborate to solve problems too complex for a single agent, each handling part of a larger workflow. `/discover/what-is-a-multi-agent-system` |
| **Agentic AI** | An advanced form of AI focused on autonomous decision-making and action: it sets goals, plans, and executes with minimal human input via a loop of Perception, Reasoning, Planning, Action, Reflection. A subset of generative AI that orchestrates agents using an LLM as the "brain". `/discover/what-is-agentic-ai` |
| **Agentic coding** | Using autonomous AI agents to plan, write, test, and refine software with limited human intervention, going beyond single-prompt code completion. `/discover/what-is-agentic-coding` |
| **Human in the loop** | A design pattern that keeps people in AI workflows to review, correct, or approve outputs, improving safety, accuracy, and accountability. `/discover/human-in-the-loop` |
| **Model Context Protocol (MCP)** | An open protocol that standardises how AI applications connect to external tools, data sources, and systems, so agents can access context in a consistent way. `/discover/what-is-model-context-protocol` |
| **Vibe coding** | An emerging, conversational style of software development where you describe intent in natural language and an AI generates and iterates the code. `/discover/what-is-vibe-coding` |

