## 8. ML operations & infrastructure

| Concept | One-line definition |
|---|---|
| **MLOps** | Practices for managing the ML life cycle from development to deployment and monitoring, bridging data science and operations. Core tasks: experiment tracking, deployment, monitoring, retraining. `/discover/what-is-mlops` |
| **LLMOps** | MLOps specialised for large language models: prompt management, evaluation, fine-tuning, deployment, and monitoring of LLM-based applications. `/discover/what-is-llmops` |
| **AIOps** | Applying ML and NLP to automate and improve IT operations, ingesting logs/metrics/events to detect anomalies, find root cause, and predict issues. Process: Observe, Engage, Act. `/discover/what-is-aiops` |
| **Kubeflow** | An open-source platform for running ML workflows (pipelines, training, serving) portably on Kubernetes. `/discover/what-is-kubeflow` |
| **GPU for AI** | The massively parallel processor that is the hardware workhorse of AI, accelerating both training and inference far beyond sequential CPUs. `/discover/gpu-for-ai` |
| **TPU (Tensor Processing Unit)** | Google's custom ASIC purpose-built to accelerate ML workloads at scale; powers Gemini and Google products via matrix-multiply units. (Product page.) `/tpu` |
| **High performance computing (HPC)** | Aggregating compute (clusters, accelerators, fast interconnects) to run massive parallel workloads, including large-scale AI training. `/discover/what-is-high-performance-computing` |
| **AI workload** | The compute, data, and model tasks involved in training, tuning, and serving AI, and the infrastructure needed to run them. `/discover/what-is-an-ai-workload` |
| **Data labeling** | Annotating raw data with meaningful labels (the training data for supervised ML). Done manually, automatically, or hybrid; quality labels improve accuracy and reduce bias. `/use-cases/data-labeling` |

